        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "f652a4fe-104f-5701-a3e1-cec3f312081c",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Sylvia Plath, Ted Hughes, Michael Cunningham\n- Works: The Bell Jar, The Hours\n- Places: Boston, Smith College, Newnham College, University of Cambridge, United States, England\n- Topics: poetry, novels, short stories, clinical depression, electroconvulsive therapy, suicide, literary awards\n\nFacts:\n- Sylvia Plath was born in Boston on October 27, 1932, and she was an American poet, novelist, and short story writer.\n- Sylvia Plath studied at Smith College and at Newnham College of the University of Cambridge before gaining acclaim as a poet and writer.\n- The Bell Jar is the only novel written by Sylvia Plath.\n- Sylvia Plath married fellow poet Ted Hughes in 1956, and they lived together in the United States and later in England.\n- Sylvia Plath and Ted Hughes had two children, Frieda and Nicholas, before separating in 1962.\n- Sylvia Plath experienced clinical depression for most of her adult life and received electroconvulsive therapy multiple times.\n- Sylvia Plath died by suicide on February 11, 1963.\n- Michael Cunningham was born on November 6, 1952, and he is a U.S. novelist and screenwriter.\n- Michael Cunningham is best known for the 1998 novel The Hours, which won the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction and the PEN/Faulkner Award in 1999.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
